<Guide
  title="Linux Note-Taking App for Handwriting & PDFs | Myelin Notes"
  heading="Handwriting and PDF notes on Linux"
  description="Myelin Notes brings handwriting, typed notes, and PDF annotation to a native Linux app. Keep your work on your computer and edit without an internet connection."
>
  <h2>A canvas for lecture notes, papers, and sketches</h2>
  <p>
    A lecture rarely fits into just typed paragraphs. You might need to sketch a
    diagram, circle an equation in a paper, and write a summary beside it. Myelin
    Notes keeps those pieces together: ink, text, images, and PDFs share the same
    canvas, so you can arrange a note around the material you are studying.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <Shot src="/pdf.png" alt="Myelin Notes canvas with a PDF equation boxed in green ink and an arrow drawn beside the paper" eager />
    <figcaption>PDF markup and freehand ink share one canvas. App shown on macOS.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Write with a pen, then switch to the keyboard</h2>
  <p>
    Use the pen tool for handwriting and sketches, the highlighter to mark a
    passage, and the eraser to revise your ink. The pen supports pressure-sensitive
    strokes when your tablet, Linux drivers, and input system provide pressure
    data. You can also draw with a mouse or turn pressure off for uniform strokes.
  </p>
  <p>
    For longer explanations, add a text box or a page frame. Page frames support
    headings, lists, checkboxes, tables, math, and Markdown shortcuts. A typed
    summary can sit beside your handwritten working instead of replacing it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Handwriting recognition is currently available on macOS only. You can write
    and draw on Linux, but handwriting OCR is not available there. Pen behavior
    also depends on your hardware and drivers; try a short note on your own setup.
  </p>

  <h2>Annotate a PDF without leaving your notes</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Download the Linux AppImage from the Myelin Notes releases page. Allow the file to run as a program in your file manager, then launch it.</li>
    <li>Create a note and drop a PDF onto the canvas.</li>
    <li>Draw on the PDF to circle a detail or highlight a passage. Add your own explanation beside the document.</li>
    <li>Export the annotated PDF when you need to share your markup outside Myelin Notes.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    For example, keep a research paper in the center of a note, mark the equations
    you want to revisit, and use the space beside it for derivations and questions.
    The original document and your working stay together as you study.
  </p>

  <h2>Use Linux alongside Mac or Windows</h2>
  <p>
    Myelin Notes runs on Linux, Mac, and Windows. Editing works offline; optional
    GitHub sync connects a workspace through a repository you control. See the
    <a href="/guides/offline-notes/">offline note-taking guide</a> for what needs a
    connection and what stays available without one. iPhone, iPad, and Android
    apps are still coming soon.
  </p>
  <p>
    If your existing notes are in OneNote, you can
    <a href="/guides/import-onenote/">import a OneNote notebook or section</a>.
    Myelin Notes is free for personal use. Its source is public under PolyForm
    Strict 1.0.0; commercial use requires a separate license.
  </p>
</Guide>
